commit b21821d6acbc9e1bef8d9d71293bf7cee19a68f1
Author: SouvikSarkar011008 <souviksarkar011008@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Oct 20 08:48:05 2026 +0530

    fix: the unix-only test imports were removed by a Windows-only lint
    
    The imports of MAX_BLOCK_BYTES, MAX_BLOCK_LINES and Value in the repl
    onboarding tests are used by code that is cfg(unix) and
    cfg(target_os = "linux"). On Windows none of that code is compiled, so
    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings correctly reported all three as
    unused - on a Windows machine. Removing them broke the LINUX build: the rbops
    run died in its "Warm cargo cache" step with "could not compile redblue (lib
    test)" and E0425/E0433 for MAX_BLOCK_BYTES.
    
    The lint cannot see the other platform. The import can: the three now carry
    cfg(unix), so Windows does not compile them and Linux does. That is the same
    code on both platforms, with no unused import on either.
    
    This is the second time a platform-specific lint has been trusted over the
    other platform's build, and the lesson is the same: a lint that says
    "unused" on the machine you are standing on is not evidence about the
    machine CI runs on.

